   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Thank you\r\nEdward for answering. Of course I understand the difference between these two\r\ntypes of data. Nevertheless, I wonder what technical solutions underlie the cfa\r\nfunction in lavaan. I mean, does the cfa function using</span><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-family:Calibri;background-position:initial initial;background-repeat:initial initial\"> a polychoric\r\ncorrelation matrix, and diagonally weighted least squares</span><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-family:Calibri\"> </span><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-family:Calibri\">in both cases? What\r\nis the technical difference in the DWLS estimation? Is there any manual / paper\r\nin which I can read about it?<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Regards,<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Paweł<u></u><u></u></span></p></div>"
    ],
