   "subject": "Re: Difference in DWLS estimator model fit in case of numeric and ordered data",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Pawel,<br>Perhaps the source of the confusion here involves the scope of DWLS.  Today, DWLS is perhaps most commonly used in conjunction with threshold models for ordinal data.  However, it was developed independently and, I believe, earlier.  If I am not mistaken, DWLS was introduced into LISREL as a computationally less demanding alternative to WLS (ADF).  Both were presented as methods for modeling non-normal continuous data.  In this respect, DWLS differs from mean-adjusted and mean-and-variance-adjusted WLS which I believe were both developed specifically in the context of threshold models for categorical data.<br><br>So, when you choose DWLS in Lavaan, you are not automatically choosing to use threshold models.  You are simply choosing an estimation technique for the model that you specified.  You can confirm this by looking at the parameters listed in the summary output or parameter matrices. If you use DWLS to estimate a continuous variable model, you will not see thresholds among the parameters.<br><br># The Holzinger and Swineford (1939) example<br>HS.model &lt;- &#39; visual  =~ x1 + x2 + x3<br>              textual =~ x4 + x5 + x6<br>              speed   =~ x7 + x8 + x9 &#39;<br><br>fit &lt;- lavaan(HS.model, data=HolzingerSwineford1939,<br>              auto.var=TRUE, auto.fix.first=TRUE,<br>              auto.cov.lv.x=TRUE, estimator=&#39;DWLS&#39;)<br><br>summary(fit)<br>lavInspect(object=fit, what=&#39;free&#39;)<br><br>Keith<br>------------------------<br>Keith A. Markus<br>John Jay College of Criminal Justice, CUNY<br><a href=\"http://jjcweb.jjay.cuny.edu/kmarkus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://jjcweb.jjay.cuny.edu/kmarkus&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792426537607000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3fexKX77v_FQC6W0NwkRXh\">http://jjcweb.jjay.cuny.edu/kmarkus</a><br>Frontiers of Test Validity Theory: Measurement, Causation and Meaning.<br><a href=\"http://www.routledge.com/books/details/9781841692203/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.routledge.com/books/details/9781841692203/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792426537607000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0CoZCQc0kdI1lY9XT6VoU-\">http://www.routledge.com/books/details/9781841692203/</a><br><br></div>"
    ]
   ]
